    "description": "Banking customer management connects verified identity, account relationships, and information maintained throughout a customer's relationship with a bank. In Iran, published regulatory material links account-based services to national customer-identification infrastructure, although current implementation requires further verification.",
    "tags": ["Customer management", "Customer due diligence", "Banking data", "Identity verification", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:28:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:28:43Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Banking Customer Management in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Customer identity, ongoing due diligence, and reliable banking records, with clearly stated gaps in current Iranian implementation evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "identity-and-relationship",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the customer relationship",
        "content": "Customer management in banking involves more than maintaining a name and contact address. The Financial Action Task Force, the intergovernmental body that sets international standards against money laundering and terrorist financing, distinguishes identifying a customer, verifying representatives, identifying beneficial owners, and understanding the intended relationship. A beneficial owner is the individual who ultimately owns or controls the customer. [s1]\n\nThese distinctions are especially useful for understanding business accounts: the company, its representative, and its ultimate owner need not be the same person. The international framework is an explanatory benchmark here; it does not establish Iranian legal requirements or demonstrate compliance by an Iranian institution. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-identification",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Iran's published identification framework",
        "content": "A reproduced Iranian directive on banking-transaction transparency, identified as adopted on Solar Hijri 1401/12/27, states that account-based banking operations depend on obtaining a Shahab identifier through the Nahab system. In this context, Nahab is the system used to obtain that customer identifier. The reproduced text also says that obtaining Shahab does not remove a bank's other customer-identification responsibilities. [s2]\n\nThis is evidence of a published rule's wording, not proof of its present enforcement. The inspected material was a legal reproduction rather than an authenticated Central Bank publication. Subsequent amendments, exceptions, and current operational procedures remain unverified. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ongoing-information",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping information useful over time",
        "content": "International due-diligence standards describe an ongoing process: transactions are considered against knowledge of the customer's business and risk profile, and relevant records are reviewed and updated. Successful onboarding therefore does not make customer information permanently complete. [s1]\n\nReliable records also require consistent definitions and reconciliation. Basel's risk-data principles recommend an authoritative source for each type of risk data and a shared dictionary of concepts. Applied to customer information, this suggests an evaluation question: can the bank explain how records used in lending and risk reporting refer to the same customer? This is an editorial inference from the guidance, not a verified description of Iranian systems. [s3]\n\nThe central distinction is between possessing an identifier and maintaining a well-supported understanding of a relationship. Publication of a fuller Iran-specific account requires authoritative rules and documented implementation evidence.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
