    "description": "Bank treasury systems bring together information about cash obligations, funding, and collateral to support liquidity decisions. For Iran, historical evidence explains why funding arrangements matter, but current operating rules and bank-level deployments require further verification.",
    "tags": ["Bank treasury", "Liquidity management", "Funding", "Collateral", "Risk data", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:28:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:28:43Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Bank Treasury Systems in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to bank liquidity, funding, collateral, and treasury data, distinguishing general principles from verified Iranian practice."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "liquidity-purpose",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Having funds available when obligations fall due",
        "content": "Liquidity concerns a bank's ability to meet cash and collateral obligations without unacceptable losses. The US Federal Reserve describes liquidity-risk management as anticipating funding needs and coordinating available resources under normal and stressed conditions. This definition helps explain the purpose of treasury systems; it is not an Iranian regulatory requirement. [s1]\n\nA treasury view must consider timing as well as amounts. An expected receipt later in the day may not cover a payment due earlier. Similarly, an asset's recorded value does not by itself establish whether cash can be raised from it when needed. These are practical implications of managing cash and collateral obligations across time. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "funding-and-collateral",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting forecasts, funding, and collateral",
        "content": "Basel liquidity guidance emphasizes cash-flow projections, funding concentrations, collateral, stress tests, and contingency funding plans. It also distinguishes intraday needs from longer funding horizons. A useful conceptual treasury system therefore connects expected flows with available resources and the conditions under which those resources can be used. [s2]\n\nThis does not mean every forecast is certain. Normal assumptions and stressed assumptions serve different purposes. The diagram below summarizes an analytical relationship, rather than prescribing a transaction sequence or documenting an Iranian implementation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "liquidity-analysis",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From liquidity information to a funding response",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Expected cash inflows and outflows\"] -->|\"Inform\"| C[\"Liquidity assessment\"]\n  B[\"Available funding and collateral\"] -->|\"Constrain feasible responses\"| C\n  D[\"Stress assumptions\"] -->|\"Test resilience\"| C\n  C -->|\"Identifies potential shortfalls\"| E[\"Funding response and contingency plan\"]\n```",
        "summary": "Cash-flow expectations, usable resources, and stress assumptions inform liquidity assessment. The arrows show analytical inputs and outputs, not legal authority or a guaranteed ability to obtain funding.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-and-data",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Iranian context and the limits of software",
        "content": "In its 2018 Iran consultation, IMF staff described liquidity-management plans associated with access to central-bank emergency lending and recommended changes to lending facilities. These observations explain the historical importance of funding arrangements, but they should not be read as current facility terms. [s3]\n\nData reliability is equally important. A Basel Committee newsletter published in January 2026 identifies persistent international challenges involving fragmented systems, data lineage, and timely reporting. Data lineage means being able to trace information from its origin to its final use. The newsletter reports international experience, not an assessment of Iran. [s4]\n\nFor this page, the resulting evaluation question is whether a treasury report can explain its inputs, assumptions, and constraints. A dashboard alone cannot demonstrate that funding will remain available in stress. Current Iranian market-operation rules, collateral conditions, and documented bank practices are needed to complete the local account. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
